    {
      "type": "concept",
      "title": "2009: Gordon Brown's Apology",
      "content": "The first official break came in August 2009, when computer scientist John Graham-Cumming launched an online petition asking the British government to apologize for Turing's treatment. Within weeks it had over 30,000 signatures. On September 10, 2009, Prime Minister Gordon Brown responded with a statement published on the Downing Street website that went far further than the petition had asked. 'Thousands of people have come together to demand justice for Alan Turing and recognition of the appalling way he was treated,' Brown wrote. 'While Turing was dealt with under the law of the time, and we cannot put the clock back, his treatment was of course utterly unfair, and I am pleased to have the chance to say how deeply sorry I and we all are for what happened to him.' Brown explicitly placed Turing 'among those few of whom we can say, without their unique contribution history would have been a different and a darker place.' It was the first time a British prime minister had publicly apologized for the prosecution of a single citizen under a now-repealed law. It set the precedent for everything that followed.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "diagram",
        "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"36\" fill=\"#ffd700\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">The Apology, September 10, 2009</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"24\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Prime Minister Gordon Brown, Downing Street website</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"170\" width=\"940\" height=\"200\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"10\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"210\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#6366f1\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">The Petition</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"250\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Launched August 2009 by John Graham-Cumming</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"285\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">British computer scientist and author</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"320\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Over 30,000 signatures in weeks</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"355\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Asked simply for an apology — got more than expected</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"410\" width=\"940\" height=\"310\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"10\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"450\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#ffd700\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">The Statement</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"495\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-style=\"italic\">\"Thousands of people have come together</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"525\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-style=\"italic\">to demand justice for Alan Turing.\"</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"575\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-style=\"italic\">\"His treatment was of course utterly unfair.\"</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"625\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-style=\"italic\">\"On behalf of the British government,</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"655\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-style=\"italic\">and all those who live freely thanks to Alan's work,</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"685\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-style=\"italic\">I am very proud to say: we're sorry.\"</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"740\" width=\"940\" height=\"160\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"10\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"780\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#10b981\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">What Was Unprecedented</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"820\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">First PM apology for a named gay conviction</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"855\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Not just regret — explicit acknowledgement of injustice</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"885\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Published on the official Downing Street website</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"920\" width=\"940\" height=\"110\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"10\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"960\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">The Apology Was Not Enough</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"1000\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">The conviction itself remained on the books</text></svg>",
        "caption": "Gordon Brown's 2009 statement: the first formal British apology for prosecuting a named citizen."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "concept",
      "title": "2013: The Royal Pardon",
      "content": "An apology is a statement of regret. A pardon is a legal act. In 2011, peer Lord Sharkey began a campaign in the House of Lords for a posthumous pardon of Turing's 1952 conviction for gross indecency under Section 11 of the Criminal Law Amendment Act 1885 — the same statute under which Oscar Wilde had been jailed in 1895. The bill was initially opposed by the Conservative justice minister Lord McNally, who argued that Turing had been 'properly convicted' under the law of the time and that selective posthumous pardons were inappropriate. Public pressure mounted. In July 2013 a Royal Pardon was finally agreed; Queen Elizabeth II signed it on December 24, 2013, under the Royal Prerogative of Mercy. The pardon took effect immediately. It was the fourth posthumous royal pardon granted since the Second World War. Justice Secretary Chris Grayling, who had recommended the pardon, said: 'Dr. Turing deserves to be remembered and recognised for his fantastic contribution to the war effort, and his legacy to science. A pardon from the Queen is a fitting tribute to an exceptional man.'",
      "visual": {
        "type": "diagram",
        "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"36\" fill=\"#ffd700\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">The Royal Pardon</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"24\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Queen Elizabeth II, December 24, 2013</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"170\" width=\"940\" height=\"200\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"10\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"210\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#6366f1\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">The Lords Campaign, 2011-2013</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"250\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Led by Lord Sharkey (Liberal Democrat peer)</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"285\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Backed by Stephen Hawking, Lord Rees, Royal Society</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"320\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Initial government objection: \"properly convicted\"</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"355\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Public pressure overcame ministerial reluctance</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"410\" width=\"940\" height=\"200\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"10\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"450\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">The Pardon Itself</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"490\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Granted under the Royal Prerogative of Mercy</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"525\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Signed by Queen Elizabeth II on December 24, 2013</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"560\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Took effect immediately</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"595\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Only the fourth such posthumous pardon since 1945</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"650\" width=\"940\" height=\"180\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"10\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"690\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#10b981\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">The Statute</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"730\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Section 11, Criminal Law Amendment Act 1885</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"765\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">The same law used against Oscar Wilde in 1895</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"800\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Repealed in England and Wales by the Sexual Offences Act 1967</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"850\" width=\"940\" height=\"180\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"10\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"890\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#ffd700\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Chris Grayling's Statement</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"935\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-style=\"italic\">\"Dr. Turing deserves to be remembered and recognised</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"965\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-style=\"italic\">for his fantastic contribution to the war effort.\"</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"1010\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Justice Secretary on the day of the pardon</text></svg>",
        "caption": "A Christmas Eve royal pardon: rare, legal, and 59 years late."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "title": "The Turing Law of 2017 and the £50 Note of 2021",
      "content": "Turing's pardon raised an immediate moral question: what about the estimated 49,000 other men, mostly anonymous, convicted under the same Victorian laws between 1885 and 1967? Many were still alive. In 2016, in response to sustained campaigning, the British government announced the Policing and Crime Act 2017, popularly known as the Alan Turing Law, which extended a posthumous pardon to all deceased men convicted of consensual gay sex acts under historical laws, and provided a disregard process for living men still carrying such convictions. By 2021 over 600 living men had had their records formally cleared. Then in 2019, the Bank of England announced that Turing would appear on its new polymer £50 note, replacing James Watt and Matthew Boulton. The note entered circulation on June 23, 2021 — Turing's 109th birthday. Its design features a young Turing, the ACE pilot computer, the Bombe, the Turing Test, a foretaste-of-what-is-to-come quote, and a tabulation of the binary representation of the date. A man prosecuted for his sexuality in 1952, dead in 1954, hidden by the Official Secrets Act for thirty years, was now the face on Britain's highest-denomination banknote. The arc was complete.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "diagram",
        "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"36\" fill=\"#ffd700\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">The Turing Law and the £50 Note</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"24\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Pardon, repair, recognition</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"170\" width=\"940\" height=\"240\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"10\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"210\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#6366f1\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">2017 — The Alan Turing Law</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"250\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Part of the Policing and Crime Act 2017</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"285\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Posthumous pardons for ~49,000 dead men</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"320\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Disregard process for living men's records</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"355\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Convictions under the 1885 Act and successors</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"395\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Turing's pardon became a template for thousands</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"430\" width=\"940\" height=\"200\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"10\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"470\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">2019 — Bank of England Selection</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"510\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">227,000 public nominations for £50 face</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"545\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Shortlist: 12 scientists</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"580\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Selected by Governor Mark Carney, July 2019</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"615\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Replaced steam-engine pioneers Watt and Boulton</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"650\" width=\"940\" height=\"230\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"4\" rx=\"15\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"695\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"30\" fill=\"#ffd700\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">£50 — June 23, 2021</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"735\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Issued on Turing's 109th birthday</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"770\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Photograph: portrait, 1951, NPL collection</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"805\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Design includes: ACE, Bombe rotors, ticker-tape binary date</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"840\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Quote: \"Only a foretaste of what is to come\"</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"870\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Britain's first openly gay person on a banknote</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"900\" width=\"940\" height=\"130\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"10\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"940\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#10b981\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Statue Manchester, 2001 — Statue Bletchley, 2007</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"980\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Sackville Gardens bronze, Manchester (Glyn Hughes)</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"1015\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Slate statue at Bletchley Park (Stephen Kettle)</text></svg>",
        "caption": "Turing Law, banknote, statues: a country writing the man back into its identity."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "quiz",
      "question": "Which of the following accurately describes the 'Alan Turing Law' (Policing and Crime Act 2017)?",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "It granted posthumous pardons to all deceased men convicted under historical laws criminalizing consensual gay sex, and created a disregard process for living men still bearing such records",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "text": "It granted Alan Turing alone a royal pardon for his 1952 conviction",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "It outlawed all future criminal charges based on a defendant's sexual orientation",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "text": "It declassified all wartime Bletchley Park documents related to Turing's cryptanalysis",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "The 2017 Alan Turing Law extended Turing's individual 2013 royal pardon into a general principle. It granted automatic posthumous pardons to the estimated 49,000 deceased men convicted of consensual same-sex offences under historical statutes (chiefly Section 11 of the Criminal Law Amendment Act 1885 and the Sexual Offences Act 1956), and created a 'disregard' application process by which living men could have those convictions formally erased from their criminal records. Turing's own pardon had been granted earlier, in 2013, by direct Royal Prerogative of Mercy.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "diagram",
        "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"36\" fill=\"#ffd700\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">One Pardon, Then Tens of Thousands</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"24\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">The arc from 2013 to 2017</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"170\" width=\"940\" height=\"180\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"10\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"210\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#6366f1\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">2013 — Turing's Pardon Alone</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"250\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Royal Prerogative of Mercy</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"285\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Individual, named, specific</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"320\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Critics: why only him?</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"370\" width=\"940\" height=\"260\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"10\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"410\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#10b981\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">2017 — The Alan Turing Law</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"450\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Section 165 of the Policing and Crime Act 2017</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"485\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Posthumous pardon: ~49,000 deceased men</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"520\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Disregard process: living men can clear records</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"555\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Covered the 1885 Act and its successors</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"595\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Extended Scottish and Northern Irish equivalents adopted in parallel</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"650\" width=\"940\" height=\"180\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\" stroke=\"#ec4899\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"10\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"690\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#ec4899\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">What the Law Does Not Do</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"730\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Does not pardon non-consensual offences</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"765\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Does not pardon offences still criminal today</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"800\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Each case reviewed against modern law</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"850\" width=\"940\" height=\"180\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"10\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"890\" font-family=\"serif\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Why Turing's Name Carried</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"930\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">His specific pardon set a moral precedent</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"965\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">If he should be pardoned, why not the others?</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"1005\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#10b981\" text-anchor=\"middle\">The argument that made the general law impossible to refuse</text></svg>",
        "caption": "From one named pardon to a general statute: Turing's case opened a door, and 49,000 walked through."
      }
    }
